# __SLUG__

`pswamp-__SLUG__`, package `pswamp_modules.__PKG__`, class `__NAME__Source`,
entry point `__SLUG__` (group `pswamp.modules`).

The __LABEL__ source. A **source** has no inputs: it produces. This one is a
*history* of synthetic frames (two stations at 20 Hz, a slow swing around
50 Hz), a placeholder to replace with a read of your own data.
It is a `SourceModule` (a `Module` with no inputs); whether it can be replayed paced and sought, as the
player does, depends on its `Playable` mixin (see below).

| | Message | Topic | Defined in |
|---|---|---|---|
| Reads | nothing | | |
| Emits | `PmuFrame` (the primary stream) | `pmu.frame` | `pswamp_models.pmu` |

**Settings:** `seconds`, how much synthetic data it holds (default 3). A
deployment sets `{SOURCE}_SECONDS` (`__UPPER___SECONDS` for the source named
`__SLUG__` in `pipelines/__SLUG__.toml`); a script passes
`__NAME__Source(seconds=10)`. Declare your own in `env_settings`.

## From a script

`read` is a plain generator: no server, no transport, no event loop.

```python
from pswamp_modules.__PKG__ import __NAME__Source

source = __NAME__Source()
frames = list(source.read())              # every frame, in order
chunk = list(source.read(start, end))     # a half-open range [start, end)
source.coverage()                         # what it holds: [first, end)
```

The host calls `aread` / `acoverage`, derived from the same code. A source that
talks to something asynchronous implements `aread` / `acoverage` instead and
gets the synchronous forms for free.

## Replayable, or live

- A history becomes **replayable** (play, pause, step, seek, speed, loop, as the
  streamer's recording is) by adding the `Playable` mixin to its bases:
  `class __NAME__Source(Playable, SourceModule)`.
  `pswamp new module <name> <label> --source --playable` generates it that way.
- A **live** feed sets `kind = "live"`, has no `coverage`, and yields frames
  stamped now as they arrive, until `end` or for ever. It is not `Playable`:
  `modules/live-synthetic/` is the example.

## Using it in a pipeline

`pipelines/__SLUG__.toml` names it in `[[sources]]` and reads it with the frame
statistics module. `__UPPER___SOURCES` (`name:entry-point,...`) replaces the list in
a deployment. `uv run pswamp modules list` shows what is installed.

## Examples

`examples/read___PKG__.py` reads the source from a plain script:

    uv run python modules/__SLUG__/examples/read___PKG__.py

## Tests

`uv run pswamp test module __SLUG__` (also part of `uv run pswamp test server`).
The source passes `pswamp_core.testing.SourceConformance`: the contract every
source keeps (ordered UTC timestamps, half-open ranges, a seek lands on the
record, a stream closed early can be read again, sync and async reads agree).
